1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.370
In the previous lessons, you constrained what your agent could show

2
00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.874
through specific components in the case of controlled generative UI,

3
00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:10.477
or through a catalog of building blocks

4
00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:12.879
in the case of declarative generative UI.

5
00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.482
In this lesson, you'll take a different approach

6
00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:17.684
by removing these constraints on your agent's UI.

7
00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.387
First, you'll connect your agent to MCP apps,

8
00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:22.989
letting it hand off control to third-party applications.

9
00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.091
The same applications that are built for

10
00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:27.594
the ChatGPT and Claude App stores.

11
00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:31.398
We will follow up by exploring fully open-ended generative UI,

12
00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:33.199
where you allow your agent to

13
00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:35.301
create brand new interfaces on the fly.

14
00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:38.705
Fully open-ended Gen UI really captures the full promise

15
00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:42.108
of how Gen UI could continue to evolve. Let's get coding.

16
00:00:42.142 --> 00:00:43.910
The first type of Open-Ended Gen

17
00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.979
UI we will explore is MCP Apps.

18
00:00:46.346 --> 00:00:48.948
Instead of building a UI for every new capability,

19
00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:51.751
with MCP Apps, you simply connect your full-stack agents

20
00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:53.953
to third-party applications that already exist

21
00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:56.156
and which come with their own UI and branding.

22
00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.958
As a developer, you only need to wire up the MCP Apps,

23
00:00:59.159 --> 00:01:00.960
which are then made available to the agent.

24
00:01:01.061 --> 00:01:02.662
The agent then decides when to call

25
00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:04.697
on those apps and how to interact with them.

26
00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:06.733
MCP Apps are the standard supported

27
00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:08.835
by the ChatGPT and Claude app stores,

28
00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:10.837
which means you can bring the same applications

29
00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:13.640
that your users are using in these environments

30
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.842
to your own custom agentic applications,

31
00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:19.312
including Figma, HubSpot, Excalidraw, and so on.

32
00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:22.916
MCP Apps are implemented via a three-part architecture.

33
00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:25.618
The first part is the MCP server.

34
00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:28.421
With the MCP apps extension to the MCP protocol,

35
00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:32.425
the same server that serves your familiar MCP tools

36
00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:34.828
can now also serve UI resources.

37
00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:37.530
These UI resources can then be assembled

38
00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.732
into an iframe web representation.

39
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.302
Second, you have your host front-end application.

40
00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:46.306
The UI resources are embedded inside of a double iframe

41
00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:48.508
for isolation and security purposes.

42
00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:52.512
The third part is of course the MCP app view itself.

43
00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:56.015
This MCP app view is isolated from the rest of the application,

44
00:01:56.015 --> 00:01:59.519
and it may communicate with the host front end via JSON-RPC.

45
00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:01.354
It's worth noting that even though

46
00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:03.523
MCP apps are served by MCP servers,

47
00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:06.025
they are otherwise completely independent from the

48
00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:08.528
MCP tools returned by those same servers.

49
00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:11.498
This may be a little confusing because MCP apps evolved

50
00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:15.502
out of a previous standard called MCPUI, which did allow any

51
00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:19.272
MCP tool to optionally return UI resources from any interaction.

52
00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:22.675
But MCP UI has now evolved into the MCP Apps standard,

53
00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:25.345
which again, has fully decoupled the

54
00:02:25.345 --> 00:02:28.081
UI resources aspect from the MCP tools.

55
00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:30.283
The tools and the UI are served by

56
00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:32.385
the same servers, but they do not otherwise interact.

57
00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:36.789
The main reason to use MCP Apps is to bring third-party applications

58
00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.392
from those ChatGPT and Claude App Stores

59
00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:41.794
into your own custom agentic applications.

60
00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:45.565
MCP apps are highly optimized for that specific use case,

61
00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:49.969
including with its benefits, which allow fully custom branding from the outside,

62
00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:53.773
and interaction with third-party services at the expense of some tradeoffs.

63
00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:55.608
MCP apps are essentially an app

64
00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:57.777
designed to live inside of another app,

65
00:02:57.777 --> 00:02:59.879
which introduces meaningful indirection overhead.

66
00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:03.950
So you typically would not want to implement your own app's functionality

67
00:03:04.117 --> 00:03:07.921
as MCP apps. In a moment, we're going to embed

68
00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:10.623
the Excalidraw MCP server into your application,

69
00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:14.627
which will expose the Excalidraw MCP app to your full-stack agent.

70
00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:16.930
As you can see, there's just a single

71
00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.232
MCP server URL you have to deal with.

72
00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:22.135
There's no separate URL specifically for the MCP app.

73
00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:25.605
Finally, at the far end of the Generative UI spectrum,

74
00:03:25.605 --> 00:03:27.607
we find fully open-ended Generative UI,

75
00:03:27.607 --> 00:03:30.610
where your agent writes brand new applications on the fly,

76
00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.413
typically to be embedded inside of the AI chat.

77
00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:35.181
This part of the Gen UI

78
00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:36.950
spectrum, of course, provides the most flexibility.

79
00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.552
You're essentially giving your agent full creative freedom.

80
00:03:39.953 --> 00:03:41.721
But this comes at a tradeoff.

81
00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:43.756
Generation is slower and more error prone.

82
00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:46.259
It is far less consistent and deterministic,

83
00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:48.361
and the quality of your result

84
00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.563
is highly sensitive both to model choice

85
00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:52.031
and even to the prompts and

86
00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:53.766
skills that you feed to your agent.

87
00:03:53.967 --> 00:03:57.770
Fully open-ended gen UI is still in the exploratory phase of adoption.

88
00:03:57.971 --> 00:04:00.173
But as the AI stack continues to evolve,

89
00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:02.475
it will likely fill a larger and larger role

90
00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:04.577
in the production stack over time.

91
00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:06.779
All right, let's get to the code. Just like before,

92
00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:08.982
we'll start with the reset lesson cell.

93
00:04:09.215 --> 00:04:11.618
to make sure our notebook is in a clean state.

94
00:04:11.818 --> 00:04:15.221
And just like before, we'll begin by setting up our project dependencies.

95
00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:18.024
We'll begin by installing the Python requirements,

96
00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:24.731
followed by our frontend requirements.

97
00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:29.202
And finally, we'll load our API keys.

98
00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:32.171
They're pre-provided by the deep learning platform.

99
00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:35.408
We'll now start the backend and

100
00:04:35.408 --> 00:04:37.176
the frontend, just like we did before.

101
00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:39.779
We'll start our backend in port 8005.

102
00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:45.485
and we'll start our front end in port 3005.

103
00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:54.027
Now that our frontend is running, let's display

104
00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:55.962
it inside of the Jupyter notebook using an iframe

105
00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:58.064
for convenience. All right, you now see

106
00:04:58.064 --> 00:05:00.133
the same chat experience we defined before.

107
00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:02.635
Now let's add an MCP app to our basic chat.

108
00:05:03.036 --> 00:05:05.138
Like we discussed before, MCP apps is an extension

109
00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:09.142
to the MCP protocol that allows MCP servers to specify

110
00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:11.978
not only tools and context, but also applications

111
00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:14.647
that may be embedded inside of the chat.

112
00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:17.950
The code you're seeing here should look very familiar from before.

113
00:05:18.084 --> 00:05:20.887
There is only one point of addition we're making here.

114
00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:25.191
When we define the CopilotRuntime, we specify an mcpApps parameter.

115
00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:29.095
Inside of the mcpApps parameter, we pass an array of MCP servers

116
00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:31.597
which may serve MCP apps.

117
00:05:32.098 --> 00:05:36.502
The CopilotRuntime will automatically access these MCP servers

118
00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:39.272
and determine if there are any MCP apps served from them.

119
00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:43.176
In this example, we're specifying Excalidraw's MCP app.

120
00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:45.211
You may be familiar with Excalidraw

121
00:05:45.211 --> 00:05:47.580
as a great tool for visualizing diagrams.

122
00:05:47.914 --> 00:05:50.016
That's it. That's all we have to do.

123
00:05:50.016 --> 00:05:52.085
Let's give it a try now.

124
00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:55.088
We'll display once again the updated iframe for our frontend.

125
00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:57.423
Now our chat has an Excalidraw

126
00:05:57.423 --> 00:05:59.292
MCP app to call on as needed.

127
00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:01.294
Let's give it a prompt and see what happens.

128
00:06:01.594 --> 00:06:03.863
Show me a simple network diagram

129
00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:06.065
of three routers, two laptops,

130
00:06:06.065 --> 00:06:08.234
and a server using Excalidraw

131
00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:12.538
And there you have it, an actual Excalidraw diagram

132
00:06:12.638 --> 00:06:14.607
embedded right inside of your chat.

133
00:06:14.807 --> 00:06:16.075
As you see here, there's a few

134
00:06:16.075 --> 00:06:17.510
limitations to what we got out of this.

135
00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.712
Let's try to prompt it once again and see what we get.

136
00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:22.415
We'll ask it to add labels, titles,

137
00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:24.917
and generally make it as coherent as possible.

138
00:06:27.854 --> 00:06:29.655
All right, we're doing a little bit better.

139
00:06:29.856 --> 00:06:32.458
Our diagram now has some texts and labels and arrows.

140
00:06:32.959 --> 00:06:34.727
Um, but you see it's still not

141
00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:36.462
perfect. And this is a great demonstration

142
00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:39.966
of the tradeoffs you get with open-ended generative UI.

143
00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.502
Generation takes longer,

144
00:06:42.502 --> 00:06:44.704
is less deterministic and is more error prone.

145
00:06:44.904 --> 00:06:48.908
An important tip here is that open-ended generative UI

146
00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:51.411
is the part of the generative UI spectrum

147
00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:54.213
most sensitive to prompts and to skills.

148
00:06:54.313 --> 00:06:56.916
You can often significantly improve the performance

149
00:06:56.916 --> 00:06:59.218
you get from open-ended generative UI components

150
00:06:59.385 --> 00:07:02.321
by optimizing your prompt and your agent skills.

151
00:07:02.321 --> 00:07:04.190
Okay, now that we saw how easy it

152
00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:06.092
is to add MCP apps to our application,

153
00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:09.862
let's add fully open-ended generative UI to our application as well.

154
00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:13.866
Once again, because we're using TypeScript in our Jupyter notebook,

155
00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:16.335
we can only override the file altogether.

156
00:07:16.736 --> 00:07:18.838
Almost everything in this file is exactly the

157
00:07:18.838 --> 00:07:20.940
same as it was before, with one modification.

158
00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:24.343
We're specifying a new argument in the CopilotRuntime definition,

159
00:07:24.544 --> 00:07:26.946
openGenerativeUI and setting it to true.

160
00:07:27.146 --> 00:07:29.749
Under the hood, when we do this, an open-ended

161
00:07:29.749 --> 00:07:33.352
Generative UI middleware is injected to the AGUI stack.

162
00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:36.556
Just like with A2UI, open-ended Generative UI

163
00:07:36.756 --> 00:07:41.360
is implemented by passing an additional special tool to our backend agent.

164
00:07:41.561 --> 00:07:44.363
This is propagated via the AGUI middleware stack.

165
00:07:44.597 --> 00:07:46.466
All right, let's give it a try.

166
00:07:46.466 --> 00:07:48.601
Once again, we'll display the updated frontend

167
00:07:48.801 --> 00:07:51.103
as an iframe inside of our Jupyter notebook.

168
00:07:52.405 --> 00:07:55.808
Let's try a crazy prompt. Hey chat, make it rain tacos.

169
00:07:59.812 --> 00:08:01.314
We see here that as the app is

170
00:08:01.314 --> 00:08:02.815
building, we're seeing it displayed part by part.

171
00:08:03.182 --> 00:08:05.985
And we have here something that looks like raining tacos.

172
00:08:06.285 --> 00:08:07.854
It's a little bit odd though. This shows

173
00:08:07.854 --> 00:08:09.589
another one of the tradeoffs of open-ended generative UI.

174
00:08:09.589 --> 00:08:11.524
You never quite know what you're going to get

175
00:08:11.524 --> 00:08:13.693
on the other side. Let's try to improve the prompt.

176
00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:16.562
We'll ask the agent to use taco emojis

177
00:08:16.562 --> 00:08:18.764
that are more likely to resemble real tacos

178
00:08:18.764 --> 00:08:19.899
and to make the rain look

179
00:08:19.899 --> 00:08:21.234
a little bit more like real rain.

180
00:08:21.534 --> 00:08:23.236
All right, that's not too bad.

181
00:08:23.736 --> 00:08:26.339
There's still room for improvement probably, but it's not too bad.

182
00:08:26.539 --> 00:08:29.041
So there you have it. Open-ended Generative UI.

183
00:08:29.242 --> 00:08:32.245
To recap, we showed how we can easily integrate MCP apps

184
00:08:32.245 --> 00:08:35.348
that may be served by any MCP server into our application.

185
00:08:35.548 --> 00:08:37.550
And we showed how we can let

186
00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:39.552
the agent create fully open-ended generative UI

187
00:08:39.552 --> 00:08:41.754
on the fly to meet any prompt we throw at it.

188
00:08:41.954 --> 00:08:44.557
There are tradeoffs to this part of the generative UI spectrum.

189
00:08:44.724 --> 00:08:46.826
It is slower. It is less deterministic

190
00:08:46.826 --> 00:08:50.229
and it is more likely to result in errors.

191
00:08:50.229 --> 00:08:52.732
But it also really captures our imagination

192
00:08:52.732 --> 00:08:54.133
and points the way to where

193
00:08:54.133 --> 00:08:55.735
the Generative UI world may evolve to.

194
00:08:56.035 --> 00:08:58.237
Great job. You've now gotten experience

195
00:08:58.237 --> 00:09:00.239
across the entire Generative UI spectrum.

196
00:09:00.239 --> 00:09:02.742
All the way from fully controlled Generative UI,

197
00:09:02.742 --> 00:09:04.744
to declarative Generative UI,

198
00:09:04.744 --> 00:09:07.213
and now to fully open-ended Generative UI.
